 \label{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:11.1}
\end{equation}

Indeed the relative term for an oriented \(j\)-cell is \(\pi_a(\Sigma^j H)\), which by \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:9.7} is \(k\) when \(a=j\) and zero otherwise. The first differential is the original signed attaching degree reduced modulo \(p\): smash the attaching map with the unit and use its degree-zero quotient \(\mathbb Z\to k\). Thus the sole row is the complete cellular chain complex over \(k\), with its original incidence coefficients. The filtration has just this one row, so the all-page argument of the path-fibre companion, \cref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:section-2}, identifies its only surviving quotient in degree \(a\) with the group in \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:11.1}. On an infinite complex, each representative sphere and each nullhomotopy has compact image in a finite cell stage; filtered unions therefore commute with these groups. The identification is natural on cellular maps, and cellular approximation extends it to all maps.

The same cellular calculation identifies the right side of \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:11.1} with the level-space colimit used in \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:10.6}. One can calculate the latter by the shifted reduced singular complexes and their suspension chain maps \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:10.4}. The pair exact sequence, the prism identity and finite support show that this construction preserves cofibre sequences and increasing cell unions. On a sphere cell it gives \(k\) in its cell degree with the same positive generator. Its cell filtration consequently has exactly the same original cellular differential as \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:11.1}, and a single surviving quotient in each total degree. This proves the comparison, including the actual unit and all attaching maps. A connective spectrum can be given a connective CW replacement by adding spheres representing the missing homotopy classes, then cells killing the kernels, successively in each nonnegative degree. The relative disk extension argument is the stable version of the included CW construction; compactness of each representing sphere verifies the increasing-union step. Thus \eqref{courseIII:the-adams-tower-and-exact-inverse-limits:eq:11.1} applies to all the connective spectra in the tower, independently of the number of cells used in a replacement.

mapsI:chain:lemma:4.1.text:
Conversely define \(S(c\otimes d)\) by triangulating \(\Delta^p\times\Delta^q\) along all lattice paths from \((0,0)\) to \((p,q)\). Give a path the sign of the permutation of its horizontal and vertical steps relative to all horizontal steps followed by all vertical steps. Compose its affine simplex with \((c,d)\). Internal faces from paths interchanging adjacent unlike steps cancel; boundary faces give precisely \(\partial c\otimes d+(-1)^p c\otimes\partial d\). For \(A\), deleting a nonjoining vertex gives the matching tensor boundary and joining-vertex terms telescope. Hence both maps are chain maps, preserving augmentation.

\begin{lemma}[product chain equivalence]
\label{mapsI:chain:lemma:4.1}
\leavevmode\par
The two maps are inverse up to natural chain homotopies, preserving products of subspaces.
\end{lemma}
\Uses{the hypotheses, notation and definitions stated here; the explicit constructions and calculations in the proof}


\begin{proof}
Here is an explicit inductive construction of the needed homotopies. Chains in a convex set have a contraction to any fixed vertex: cone each singular simplex to that vertex by linear interpolation. The cone boundary identity gives \(\partial K+K\partial=1-i\epsilon\), where \(\epsilon\) is augmentation and \(i\) inserts the vertex. Tensoring two such contractions gives a contraction of their tensor complex to \(\mathbb Z\): the homotopy is \(K\otimes1+(i\epsilon)\otimes L\), with the tensor sign \((-1)^{\deg c}\) in the second term. The first identity and then the second verify this formula; its boundary sum is \(1-(i\epsilon)\otimes(j\epsilon)\).

For \(1-SA\), assume a homotopy \(h\) is already defined on lower-dimensional simplices. On the universal diagonal simplex \(\Delta^m\to\Delta^m\times\Delta^m\), form
\(z=(1-SA)\sigma-h\partial\sigma\).

courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-.text:
We prove that the positive-dimensional spheres admitting almost complex structures are exactly \(S^2\) and \(S^6\). The characteristic-class argument is the one discussed by Konstantis--Parton, Sections 3--6. We supply the needed generation step in complex K-theory by polynomial clutching, so its required integrality does not rest on an unproved appeal to Bott periodicity. The same finite linearization is developed in the CC0 supporting course \emph{K-theory for operator algebras}, lesson \emph{Bott periodicity}, \cref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-5}; its full Toeplitz proof gives periodicity for a larger class of coefficient algebras than we need here.

\subsubsection{Bundles, clutching, and based loops}
\label{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-10.1}

All bases in Sections 10.1--10.3 are compact Hausdorff spaces. For such a space \(Y\), \(K^0(Y)\) is the group completion of the direct-sum monoid of finite-rank complex vector bundles. Thus its elements are differences \([E]-[F]\), with the relation that adding the same bundle to both terms does not change the difference. Tensor product makes this a ring.

We use two elementary bundle facts, with their proofs. Every bundle \(E\) over compact \(Y\) embeds in a trivial bundle: choose finitely many trivializations and a partition of unity \(\rho_i\) subordinate to them, and send \(v\in E_y\) to the list \((\sqrt{\rho_i(y)}v_i)_i\) of its local coordinates, with zero entries off the corresponding supports. At least one \(\rho_i(y)\) is positive, so the map is injective on each fibre. Its image is a subbundle, and the orthogonal complement gives \(E\oplus E^\perp\simeq\mathbf1^N\). The orthogonal projection onto the image is continuous, as is seen in a local frame by the formula \(T(T^*T)^{-1}T^*\).

Second, bundles pulled back by homotopic maps from a compact space are isomorphic. Embed the bundle over the product with the homotopy interval in a trivial bundle as just described, and let \(p(y,t)\) be its range projection. Uniform continuity gives a finite partition of the interval on which consecutive projections differ in norm by less than one, uniformly in \(y\). For projections \(p,q\) with \(\|p-q\|<1\), the map \(q:\operatorname{im}p\to\operatorname{im}q\) is injective, since \(\|qv\|\geq(1-\|p-q\|)\|v\|\). Reversing \(p,q\) gives equal ranks, so it is an isomorphism; its inverse is continuous in local finite matrices. Composing these finite isomorphisms proves the assertion. This proof applies equally with a compact disk included in the base.

Apply these facts to a bundle on \(S^2\times Y\). Its restrictions to the closed hemispheres are pullbacks of bundles on \(Y\), by contraction of the disks. Their restrictions at one equatorial point identify those two bundles, say with \(E_0\). Adding the pullback of a complement of \(E_0\) makes both hemisphere bundles trivial. The stabilized bundle is then obtained from a clutching map

\begin{equation}
g:Y\times\mathbb T\longrightarrow GL_r(\mathbb C).

The endpoints are \(\operatorname{diag}(g,h)\) and \(\operatorname{diag}(gh,I)\). All loop values at \(1\) remain the identity.

\subsubsection{The finite polynomial reduction}
\label{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-10.2}

Regard \eqref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:eq:10.1} as a based loop in matrices over the Banach algebra \(D=C(Y)\). It is uniformly approximated by Laurent polynomial loops. Indeed convolution in the circle coordinate with the Fejér kernel
\((N+1)^{-1}|\sum_{j=0}^N e^{ij\theta}|^2\)
converges uniformly for continuous Banach-valued functions: the kernel has integral one, is nonnegative, and outside any fixed arc its bound is \(((N+1)\sin^2(\theta/2))^{-1}\). Uniform continuity controls the near arc and this bound controls its complement. The inverse of \(g\) has bounded norm by compactness. Therefore a sufficiently close Laurent approximation is joined to \(g\) by invertible straight interpolations, by the Neumann series. Replacing every interpolant \(H(z)\) by \(H(z)H(1)^{-1}\) makes the entire homotopy based.

Let \(g_0\) be the resulting based Laurent polynomial loop. Choose an integer \(a\geq0\) so that \(f(z)=z^ag_0(z)\) is a polynomial, say \(f(z)=\sum_{j=0}^d a_jz^j\), with \(f(1)=I_r\). The notation \(a_j\) denotes matrix coefficients, whereas \(a\) is the chosen Laurent exponent. Form the block matrix

\begin{equation}
\mu(f)(z)=
\begin{pmatrix}
a_0&a_1&\cdots&a_d\\

The image of \(e(y)\) is a vector bundle \(E_e\): its rank is locally constant, and a nonzero minor supplies local frames. Let \(p_e\) be its orthogonal range projection. In the orthogonal splitting \(E_e\oplus E_e^\perp\), the idempotent has matrix \(\begin{pmatrix}I&T\\0&0\end{pmatrix}\). Replacing \(T\) by \((1-t)T\) is a continuous idempotent homotopy from \(e\) to \(p_e\), and \(I-e_t+ze_t\) is invertible with inverse \(I-e_t+z^{-1}e_t\). Hence the endpoint loop multiplies by \(z\) on the actual bundle \(E_e\) and by one on its complement.

\subsubsection{Integral Chern characters on a product of two-spheres}
\label{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:section-10.3}

Let \(Q\) be the line over \(S^2=\mathbb{CP}^1\) with finite-chart frame \((1,\zeta)\) and infinity-chart frame \((1/\zeta,1)\). The finite frame equals \(\zeta\) times the infinity frame. Thus the coefficient transfer from the finite to the infinity chart is exactly the loop \(z\) used above. Put \(b=[Q]-[\mathbf1]\), keeping the subtracted rank-one class.

The preceding reduction and \eqref{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:eq:10.2} show that for every compact \(Y\), the group \(K^0(S^2\times Y)\) is generated by

\begin{equation}
\operatorname{pr}_Y^*K^0(Y)
\quad\text{and}\quad
\operatorname{pr}_{S^2}^*b\ \operatorname{pr}_Y^*K^0(Y).
\label{courseIII:almost-complex-structures-and-integrability:eq:10.6}
\end{equation}

